 the namespace level so what we  had to do was build out namespace admin  namespace editor and namespace viewer  and give those to individual groups so  we map those two groups and the team  gets that group membership that means I  can have a google group and there's some  manager that owns it and the manager  just goes adds new team members or  removes team members that leaves and  then they don't have to perform a role  binding manually this are back sync  functionality is a controller that will  watch a configuration in a CR D and the  CR D says this group gets this  permission and so then it will map that  out to the bindings by reading through  Google Groups to figure out who's in the  group another functionality that we  stuffed into this was the ability to put  service accounts in groups because  currently Google Groups don't support  that so what that means is that we can  give a service account a specific role  and then add it to a group whenever we  make a new service account so we can use  GCP service accounts to do that rather  than just kubernetes service accounts so  there's a couple things that are sort of  unsolved or you know bleeding edge  problems that we're still kind of trying  to work on so one of those is kubernetes  operators in a tendancy environment the  CR d's are global definitions and  kubernetes so you can't necessarily  allow your namespace tenants to create  CRD definitions they you so what we we  do is we as a cluster operating team  deploy CRD definitions and then allow  them to make CRD resource objects in  their namespace but that means you can  only have one sort of permutation of  that CRD at a time because CR DS weren't  designed for multi-tenancy so that means  we're effectively helping people deploy  operators also the container registry is  tenant ten  at the the level of Google projects or  GCP projects rather so if you want full  isolation between teams every team has  to manage their own GCR otherwise what  we do is we host a central one for  development purposes and give everybody  like admin to it that means they have  push and delete functionalities and then  everybody can push to that but we don't  allow pulling from that for the  production cluster so our production  cluster we only give access to service  accounts and those service accounts are  then used in continuous integration or  core building so all of the containers  that run on our production environment  we know we're pushed from CI Strad  driver logging also is tenet eyes at the  project level and because you have a  shared cluster that means that all of  the logs from all of your containers are  emitted to the same shared project which  creates a little bit of a problem  because we've only given people view  access to that project we didn't want to  give them editor access to step on each  other's toes which means they can't make  log to metric conversions or log  dashboards so in order to enable that we  had to build a fan-out system that will  take fluid fluid D stackdriver  and emits to other projects from  specific namespaces data dark metrics we  use data dog instead of sack driver for  our metrics right now and primarily that  allows us to aggregate them all but it's  also not super multi-tenant so you might  have both a fan out and a fan in problem  with both logging and metrics because  your auditors might want to see all of  them and your teams just want to see the  ones they care about so GCP has the  ability to like slurp all your logs into  one auditing project so we we use that  for auditing and then we use this other  fan-out solution for the tenants  spinnaker is another one where we had to  build our own Rob operator to make  spinnaker more isolated because we have  so many namespaces we have like 20  namespaces per cluster and maybe a dozen  clusters that means that that we are  doing a count binding and role binding  in spinnaker to isolate who can deploy  to what  spinnaker which is a little complicated  and then on resource isolation probably  one of the weaknesses of kubernetes in  general for multi-tenancy is that there  are some things that are not well  isolated yet so like CPU and memory are  great they're isolated you opt into it  and then you have that amount of  resource  it even has preemption on those but like  local disk space there's a beta feature  in kubernetes that isn't in gke yet so  we can't isolate that yet disk i/o is  completely uh nice elated in kubernetes  so if you are on spinning hard drives  which I don't recommend you can actually  knock over the docker daemon if you use  too much disk at the same time so we  switched to SSDs and that is mostly  eliminated the problem but it's it's  still uh nice elated ingress and egress  I mentioned us having solved those sort  of at the platform level but they're  also a little bit on isolated one of the  ways we're hoping to get towards  isolation in the future here is with  with Sto so SDO adds the sidecars to  each container and allows you to monitor  the ingress and egress from every  container which allows you to do sort of  traffic management but kubernetes  doesn't come with that by default so  there's a whole bunch of things we also  want to do that we haven't done just to  sort of be an example of all of the  things that you might want to consider  when you're building not just a  kubernetes cluster but a multi tenant  multi environment private cloud hybrid  network cluster so one of the things I  would recommend is to put in your  defaults and quotas before you put  tenants on your system because what  we've learned is now that we've gone and  had people on our systems we can't add  quotas without breaking them so we have  to do an analysis on like who's using  what and then make them per namespace  quotas and then add like a get-ups  approval process and all that kind of  stuff  so that's a little complicated do that  upfront we would also like to go to  project isolation instead of 