ff and then you can do  role binding against users but once your  application is running  how do your applications authorize with  each other and this is kind of a problem  everybody has to solve and there's no  sort of out-of-the-box solution that I  know of yet and two n TLS is another one  gke doesn't come with this built-in you  can use sort of a let's let's encrypt  controller solution but we wanted one  that would allow us to do both public  and private domains so then we need to  host our own CA and automate the  controller for it and then make it  different from the  like one that kind of thing plus we  don't really want to be inserting TLS  certificates into every pod we'd rather  inject to the load balancer and then  have service mesh handle the last mile  encryption that way we're not putting  secrets that can be compromised down  into the pods we have sort of a two  layer encryption scheme so basically  more of a story if you're if you're big  enough to build a platform here on top  of kubernetes which kubernetes is a  platform for building platforms then  you're probably big enough to make it  multi-tenant multi-tenancy does require  additional work on top of kubernetes and  i wouldn't necessarily say it's for  everyone but if your alternative is  alternative is multi environment or  multi instance then you're gonna have to  build some layer underneath it that  allows you to deploy a bunch of clusters  and so if you're already building a  layer you might as well make it multi  tenant instead so there's obviously  trade-offs and pros and cons so take it  with a grain of salt but that's our  experience and I think we've got it  working pretty well if you go in a car  in crews or you see those driving around  those are all talking to our cloud with  the backend running on multi-tenant  kubernetes  Thank You Carl for sharing you know the  journey of cruise automation to using  kubernetes in a multi-tenant fashion i  just want to close out with a few key  sort of takeaways so you know by  centralizing kubernetes has a lot of  like learning that you have to do to  understand how to run it well and by  specializing that knowledge with one  team that operates your clusters not  only you know builds that sort of center  of excellence which allows you to  implement the best practices across  multiple clusters but it also frees up  your developers to spend their time on  developing stuff that that adds value to  your company so I think that's some of  the key takeaways and then it gives you  the operational efficiency by letting  that specialized team of two to manage  those clusters for you and it also saves  you some infrastructure cost by  consolidating a large number of clusters  into a fewer multi-tenant clusters as  well and GK has some new features that  can help you set up that multi-tenancy  as we heard from Karl and Cruz there's  still some work to be done and you know  you you'll see those features come out  as well  [Music]
 

